WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.340
How can one green leaf cell build sugar and release energy from it?

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.380
Watch one large cell stay on screen as its parts begin working.

3
00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:14.360
Its two jobs use different cell parts, but their materials connect.

4
00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:22.137
Sunlight reaches the cell, and a green chloroplast swells into view.

5
00:00:23.417 --> 00:00:27.057
A chloroplast is a cell part that takes in light for photosynthesis.

6
00:00:28.537 --> 00:00:30.657
Carbon dioxide and water move inside.

7
00:00:32.317 --> 00:00:34.637
Sugar, called glucose, and oxygen move out.

8
00:00:35.617 --> 00:00:37.417
The sugar does not sit still.

9
00:00:38.617 --> 00:00:41.837
It moves toward a red mitochondrion, another cell part.

10
00:00:43.117 --> 00:00:46.177
Most cellular respiration happens in mitochondria.

11
00:00:47.517 --> 00:00:52.077
Respiration breaks down the sugar with oxygen, and usable energy comes out.

12
00:00:53.637 --> 00:00:55.437
Carbon dioxide and water also leave.

13
00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:01.360
What went in? A reactant is a substance entering a process.

14
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:07.080
What came out? A product is a substance leaving a process.

15
00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:13.080
Watch each product badge move to the other cell part, where it becomes a reactant.

16
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.700
The products of either process can supply reactants for the other.

17
00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:23.650
Now the sun moves toward the horizon, but the cell still needs usable energy.

18
00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.710
Which moving arrows will stop after darkness arrives?

19
00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.690
Keep the chloroplast, mitochondrion, and every material in mind.

20
00:01:38.717 --> 00:01:40.737
Darkness removes the light arrow.

21
00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:44.777
The chloroplast stops making new sugar and oxygen.

22
00:01:46.257 --> 00:01:48.877
Respiration keeps using stored sugar and oxygen.

23
00:01:50.157 --> 00:01:54.117
It keeps releasing usable energy, carbon dioxide, and water.

24
00:01:55.377 --> 00:01:56.997
The plant cell is still respiring.

25
00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:03.140
Light returns, and photosynthesis starts making new sugar and oxygen again.

26
00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.740
Respiration continues at the same time.

27
00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.340
A green leaf cell does not switch between jobs.

28
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:15.260
It can photosynthesize and respire while light is available.

29
00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:21.720
Photosynthesis adds oxygen in light; respiration uses oxygen day and night.

30
00:02:22.783 --> 00:02:25.263
Follow the material badges around the cell.

31
00:02:26.843 --> 00:02:30.143
Carbon dioxide and water can help build sugar and oxygen.

32
00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:34.243
Sugar and oxygen can then feed respiration.

33
00:02:35.763 --> 00:02:36.883
Matter can be used again.

34
00:02:38.183 --> 00:02:40.403
Energy takes a different path through the picture.

35
00:02:41.517 --> 00:02:43.197
Yellow light enters once.

36
00:02:44.437 --> 00:02:49.057
Chemical energy travels with the sugar, then becomes energy the cell can use.

37
00:02:50.297 --> 00:02:51.817
Some energy leaves as heat.

38
00:02:53.017 --> 00:02:56.017
The matter arrows loop, but energy flows one way.

39
00:02:57.067 --> 00:02:58.747
Move back outside the leaf.

40
00:03:00.007 --> 00:03:03.647
The same two jobs help explain oxygen in a pond or creek.

41
00:03:05.067 --> 00:03:09.387
Photosynthetic organisms add oxygen only while light is available.

42
00:03:11.107 --> 00:03:15.527
Plants, animals, fungi, and many microbes use oxygen through respiration.

43
00:03:16.747 --> 00:03:18.567
That use continues in darkness.

44
00:03:19.667 --> 00:03:21.847
In light, oxygen can be added.

45
00:03:23.027 --> 00:03:25.907
Through day and night, respiration keeps using it.

46
00:03:27.007 --> 00:03:29.147
Carry that pattern into ponds and creeks.
